## A. Global State Lifecycle

| State | Meaning | Entry Condition | Exit Condition | Authoritative Source | Persistent | Derived | Audit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| `INACTIVE` | No effective global policy | No enabled config, disabled config, or expired effective state | Create/configure/activate | Stored state + evaluator | Maybe | Yes | If changed intentionally |
| `CONFIGURED` | Policy exists but is not active | Admin saves valid config without activation | Schedule, activate, disable | Stored global state | Yes | No | Yes |
| `SCHEDULED` | Policy has future activation | `starts_at > now` | Activation time, update, disable | Stored timestamps + evaluator | Yes | Yes effective status | Yes on schedule/update |
| `ACTIVE` | Policy applies to selected scopes | Enabled and within active window | Expire, disable, emergency change, update | Evaluator | Stored inputs, derived effective | Yes | Yes on activation/update |
| `EXPIRING` | Optional transitional warning state | Near expiration threshold | Expired or extended | Evaluator | Not required | Yes | Optional |
| `EXPIRED` | Stored policy no longer effective | `expires_at <= now` | Restore, cleanup, new activation | Evaluator | Stored config may remain | Yes | Yes if persistent cleanup occurs |
| `INACTIVE` | Effective reset after expiry/disable | Disable or expired effective state | New config/activation | Evaluator | Maybe | Yes | Yes if admin/system action |

`EXPIRING` is not strictly necessary for correctness. It is useful only for UI/status.

